#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100688 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a16c2c4f-38c3-5e8c-8962-3ad6c956748a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7321" :question/problem "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120$, which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005$, and the carrying capacity is $a=120$. Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "29ee4686-7e38-5000-9d89-58e572f84cd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7404" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7$, and the carrying capacity is $a=8$."}]}
